deduce a linear differential equation for $w$.\nTitle: Integral geometry of Euler equations\nAuthors: Nikolai Nadirashvili, Serge Vlăduţ\nDate: Wed Aug 31 13:50:42 2016\n\nWe develop an integral geometry of stationary Euler equations defining some function $w$ on the Grassmannian of affine lines in the space. This function depends on a putative compactly supported solution $v$ of the system, and we deduce a linear differential equation for $w$. We prove also that the purported annulation of $w$ implies that locally supported solutions of the steady Euler equation in $\\mathbb R^3$ are zero.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Time almost-periodic solutions of the incompressible Euler equations (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/376678434_Time_almost-periodic_solutions_of_the_incompressible_Euler_equations)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; (Smooth stationary Euler ﬂows with compact support - Proposi- ... If d“3 or d P N is even, there exists a smooth, compactly supported ... and we have the control on the seminorms, for any n P N 0,\nlutions of the Euler equations. In d“3, this is celebrated result by Gravilov [22]\n\n(see also [13]), whereas in even dimension it has been proved in [18]. We recall the\n\nstatement of the result of the latter.\n\nProposition 2.1. (Smooth stationary Euler ﬂows with compact support - Proposi-\n\ntion 2, [18]). If d“3 or d P N is even, there exists a smooth, compactly supported\n\nsolution v p x q P C 8\n\ndiv p R d,R d q, with pressure p v p x q P C 8 p R d q, of\n\nv¨∇v`∇p v“0,div v“0, x P R d.(2.1)\n\nWithout any loss of generality, we assume that spt p v q,spt p p v q Ď B d,1 p 0 q Ă R d.\n\nThen, given S P N and for any k P N, we deﬁne the rescaled functions, for any\n\nε P p 0,1 q small enough,\n\nv k p x q:“ε p S`1 qp k´1 q v p ε´k x q, p v k p x q:“ε 2 p S`1 qp k´1 q p v p ε´k x q.\n\nA straightforward computation shows tha